-- rules/depends.rofl — WHAT BREAKS IF I DELETE THIS FILE?
--
-- The facts are scanners/depends.ts; the reasoning is here, so that a reader
-- who disagrees with an answer argues with a rule body rather than with a
-- number, and so that every row has a `why`.
--
-- THE DIRECTION IS THE THING TO GET RIGHT AND IT IS EASY TO GET BACKWARDS.
-- `edge(A, B, _)` means A NEEDS B. So the blast radius of deleting B is every
-- A that reaches B — the closure taken UPSTREAM, not downstream. Downstream is
-- the other question, `what becomes unreachable`, and it is answered separately
-- below because the two get confused every time they are not named apart.

-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ closure
--
-- THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS FILE DID NOT FIT, AND THE FAILURE IS THE USEFUL
-- PART. It computed the full closure `needs(A, B) :- needs(A, X), edge(X, B, _)`
-- and asked the blast question as a projection of it. Measured 2026-09-10 on
-- this repository's own graph — 677 artifacts, 3 208 edges — that reaches
-- 88 472 rows and then dies with `hole($rule(...), space_exhausted)` against
-- the engine's 500 000-row wall, with every query answering 0. Not a bug: the
-- closure of a graph this dense is genuinely near-quadratic (677 squared is
-- 458 329), and the join accumulator is larger than its own result.
--
-- THE MODEL WAS WRONG BEFORE THE ENGINE WAS SMALL. Nobody ever asks `what is
-- the closure`; they ask `what breaks if I delete THIS`. Seeding the walk from
-- the target makes it linear in the reachable set, and the answer to the
-- question actually asked is identical. The general form is the one this
-- repository already writes about instruments: a quantity that CONTAINS the
-- answer is not the answer, and computing it can cost more than the question.
--
-- `target[dep](X)` is supplied by the caller — one fact — so this pack is
-- inert until somebody asks something.
edb(target).

-- AND THE SECOND VERSION WAS WRONG TOO, IN A WAY ONLY A CONTROL FOUND. Seeded
-- from three targets as different as `boot.rofl`, `facts/rule-shape.rofl` and
-- `scanners/rule_shape.ts`, it answered 322 and 411 FOR ALL THREE. Identical
-- numbers for unrelated files is not a blast radius, it is a report that the
-- graph has one component — and it does, because `doc_ref` and `ledger_ref`
-- run BOTH WAYS through prose: `facts/findings.rofl` names most of the tree,
-- README names findings, so everything reaches everything through two hops of
-- text. A closure over a relation that is nearly total answers `all of it` and
-- looks like an answer.
--
-- The distinction the scanner kept separable is what rescues it: a deletion
-- breaks CODE or it makes PROSE wrong, and those want different verdicts and
-- different work. Three relations, never one:
code_edge[dep](A, B) :- edge[dep](A, B, ts_import).
code_edge[dep](A, B) :- edge[dep](A, B, reads_path).
code_edge[dep](A, B) :- edge[dep](A, B, npm_script).
code_edge[dep](A, B) :- edge[dep](A, B, ci_step).

-- `generates` IS DELIBERATELY NOT A CODE EDGE, and leaving it in was the third
-- error this model made. A generator does not BREAK when its output is
-- deleted — it recreates it. With `generates` in the closure,
-- `test/rule-shape.test.ts` appeared in the blast radius of
-- `facts/rule-shape.rofl` through `scanners/rule_shape.ts`, which is the exact
-- opposite of the truth: that test was written to re-derive from source
-- BECAUSE reading the generated pack would be green by construction, and its
-- header says so. THE EDGE WAS REAL AND ITS DIRECTION WAS WRONG, which no
-- amount of checking the edge count would have caught.
regenerates[dep](G, P) :- edge[dep](G, P, generates).

-- 1. WHAT BREAKS. Backward closure over code edges only — the build, the
--    gates, the generators. This is the number a refactor is judged by.
blast[dep](D, T) :- target[dep](T), code_edge[dep](D, T).
blast[dep](D, T) :- blast[dep](M, T), code_edge[dep](D, M).

-- 2. WHAT GOES STALE. One hop, not a closure: prose is not transitive — a
--    document naming a file that names another file says nothing about the
--    third. Treating it as transitive is exactly what produced the hairball.
stale_prose[dep](D, T) :- target[dep](T), edge[dep](D, T, doc_ref).
stale_prose[dep](D, T) :- target[dep](T), edge[dep](D, T, ledger_ref).

-- 3. WHAT IS LEFT BEHIND. Forward over code edges: reached only through the
--    target, so it becomes dead weight rather than a broken build.
downstream[dep](X, T) :- target[dep](T), code_edge[dep](T, X).
downstream[dep](X, T) :- downstream[dep](M, T), code_edge[dep](M, X).

-- ----------------------------------------------------- what nothing needs
-- An artifact nothing points at. NOT the same as `safe to delete`: an entry
-- point is an orphan by construction, and so is a document nobody cites.
depended_on[dep](B) :- edge[dep](_, B, _).
orphan[dep](X) :- artifact[dep](X, _), not depended_on[dep](X).

-- ------------------------------------------------- what only prose needs
-- THE CATEGORY THAT MATTERS FOR A CLEANUP, and the reason `doc_ref` was kept
-- separable in the scanner. If every edge into X is a mention in a document or
-- a citation in the ledger, then deleting X breaks NO CODE — it only makes
-- some prose wrong, which is a text edit and not a refactor. That is a very
-- different verdict from `orphan`, and the two are the whole cleanup triage.
code_needs[dep](B) :- edge[dep](_, B, ts_import).
code_needs[dep](B) :- edge[dep](_, B, reads_path).
code_needs[dep](B) :- edge[dep](_, B, npm_script).
code_needs[dep](B) :- edge[dep](_, B, ci_step).
code_needs[dep](B) :- edge[dep](_, B, generates).   -- a generator is a writer, but it does mean somebody owns the file

only_prose[dep](X) :- depended_on[dep](X), not code_needs[dep](X).

-- ------------------------------------------------------- what the referee runs
-- A gate that CI does not run is a gate that runs when somebody remembers,
-- which this repository has already paid for once (`witness_check.ts` exited
-- non-zero on a stale witness since the day it was written and nothing ran it).
ci_runs[dep](X) :- edge[dep](W, X, ci_step), artifact[dep](W, yml).
ci_runs[dep](X) :- ci_runs[dep](Y), edge[dep](Y, X, _).

script[dep](S) :- artifact[dep](S, script).
unrun_gate[dep](S) :- script[dep](S), not ci_runs[dep](S).

-- --------------------------------------------------------- generated packs
-- A pack with a generator and no regeneration gate is the defect already
-- recorded twice in the ledger; here it is derivable rather than remembered.
generated[dep](P) :- edge[dep](_, P, generates).
regenerated_by_test[dep](P) :- edge[dep](T, P, reads_path), test_file[dep](T).
-- `npm:test` names `test/*.test.ts` as a glob, which the scanner cannot expand
-- into per-file edges, so a test file is identified by where it LIVES. Stated
-- as a rule rather than hidden in the scanner, because it is the one place
-- this model substitutes a convention for a measurement.
test_file[dep](T) :- artifact[dep](T, ts), str_pre(T, "/") = "test", T != "test".

-- A generated pack that no test reads back is one nothing can find stale.
ungated_pack[audit](P) :- generated[dep](P), not regenerated_by_test[dep](P).

-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ counts
-- Deliberately NOT pinned as numbers here: this file is loaded against a
-- generated pack that grows with the tree, and a count over it would be
-- measuring the corpus (`f_a_pin_that_moves_with_the_corpus_is_measuring_the_corpus`).
-- test/depends.test.ts asserts identities and named sets instead.
